versions.analysis_for_next:
Version 0 scored best (69.8) by labelling a tweet positive or negative when a stance was plain, even if mild. Version 1 demanded strong feeling and pushed 82 positive and 69 negative texts into neutral (66.2). Version 2 counted any lean and pushed 69 neutral texts into positive and 49 into negative (65.0). The right line is between them, close to version 0. Neutral texts version 2 got wrong but version 1 got right:
- personal plans and logistics: going to a concert, getting in line, picking something up;
- ticket sales, 'anyone wanna buy', 'who wants to go', 'are you going?';
- event listings with prices and times;
- sports commentary and critique of players;
- debating replies;
- mild 'still waiting' remarks.
Positive and negative texts version 1 got wrong but versions 0 and 2 got right:
- openly emotive promos ('come see me!!');
- expressions of wanting or looking forward ('I want IHOP');
- RIP and condolences;
- small personal miseries marked by :(, #fml or #boring.
Version 3 uses version 0 as the base and adds these distinctions. Plans, tickets, listings and invitations stay neutral unless the author shows their own excitement or love. Emoticon or hashtag misery is negative. Commentary on athletes and debating replies stay neutral unless the tone is hostile.
